\import{ftip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\taxon{Convention}
\title{Identifiability regimes for semantic representation diagnostics}
\tag{ftip}
\tag{evaluation}
\tag{identifiability}
\tag{representation}

\p{Zhou and Klindt study labels-to-latents and latents-to-labels data-generating
directions. Their general assumptions include a fixed continuous invertible map
#{g:Z\to X}, a continuous learned encoder with #{h=f\mathbin\circ g}, a
row-centered log-linear posterior #{p(y|z)={\rm softmax}(W^*z)}, effective
dimension #{n'={\rm rank}(W^*)}, and a global population cross-entropy optimum
with linear readout #{U}. The source boundary for this note is the DGP
construction and assumptions in Sections 3.1 to 3.2, Theorems 4.1 to 4.4,
Sections 5.1 to 5.4, and Section 6, with Supplementary Assumptions 8.1 to 8.3,
of \citet{Theorems 4.1 to 4.4, Sections 3 to 5, and Section 6}{zhou2026complete}.}

\p{\strong{Source observation.} When #{m\geq n'}, Theorem 4.1 makes the
classification-relevant part of every optimum linear, #{h(z)=Az+\nu(z)} with
#{UA=W^*}; the null-readout term satisfies #{\nu(z)\in N(U)} and may remain
unconstrained. When #{m<n'}, Theorem 4.2 gives generic nonlinear,
non-identifiable optima when #{\operatorname{supp}(p_Z)} has nonempty interior
#{\Omega}, #{p_Z} is continuous and strictly positive on #{\Omega}, and every
candidate #{A} has #{N(A)\cap\Omega\ne\varnothing}. These are different
capacity regimes, not two scores for one representation.}

\p{The stronger conclusions require additional structure. In the generative
case, Assumption 8.2 takes #{Z=\mathbb R_+^n} and adds a factorized exponential
conditional, constant conditional entropy, a uniform class prior, exact capacity
#{m=n=n'},
non-negative activations, fixed within-class variance, and an energy-minimizing
global optimum. Theorem 4.3 then gives the scaled permutation
#{h(z)=DP_\sigma z}. In the discriminative case, Assumption 8.3 uses
#{A^*\in\mathbb R^{n\prime\times n}} with full row rank and
#{U^*\in\mathbb R^{|Y|\times n\prime}}, adds #{W^*=U^*A^*}, #{k}-sparse
latents, and #{\operatorname{spark}(A^*)>2k}. With #{m\geq n\prime}, let
#{P_U} be the orthogonal projector onto #{\operatorname{Row}(U)}; the
classifier-visible encoder #{P_Uh(z)=Az} has #{N(A)=N(A^*)}, so the unique #{k}-sparse solution
can recover #{z}. Axis alignment and sparse recovery are distinct routes through
distinct assumptions.}

\p{Section 5 reports finite experiments rather than a universal law. Linear
alignment uses held-out coefficient of determination for a linear decoder;
axis alignment uses the mean correlation coefficient (MCC) after Hungarian
matching recovered axes to true latents. Sparse recovery compares supervised
Lasso, dense OLS, and unsupervised sparse dictionary learning. Cross-system
correspondence uses the Maximum Matching Unit (MMU): for each source unit, the
maximum absolute Pearson correlation with any unit in a declared comparison
model and layer on a declared stimulus set. The source uses ten random seeds.
Section 6 records limits from constant conditional entropy, non-negativity,
class priors, and the assumed sparse data-generating process; signed latents do
not inherit Theorem 4.3 directly.}

\p{\strong{Finite identifiability record.} For a fixed model family,
checkpoint, task split, and probe seed, record}
##{
\mathcal D=\bigl(r,m,n',\mathcal A,[h]_{\sim_r},R^2_{\rm lin},
R^2_{\rm sparse},\operatorname{MCC}_{\rm axis},
\operatorname{MCC}_{\rm sparse},\operatorname{MMU}_{\rm cross},\mathsf E\bigr).
}
\p{Here #{r} is one declared regime: sufficient-capacity linear,
undercomplete nonlinear, generative axis-aligned, or discriminative sparse
recovery. #{\mathcal A} lists the causal direction, DGP, capacity, sparsity,
and assumptions actually held. #{R^2_{\rm lin}} is the held-out coefficient
for linear latent alignment; #{R^2_{\rm sparse}} names the decoder and target,
with supervised Lasso and dense OLS kept as separate arms. #{\operatorname{MCC}_{\rm axis}}
records the matching and correlation convention for axis recovery, while
#{\operatorname{MCC}_{\rm sparse}} records the matching convention, sparsity
level, and whether the decoder is supervised or an unsupervised dictionary.
#{\operatorname{MMU}_{\rm cross}} records the directed source and comparison
model, layers, unit sets, stimulus set, and correlation rule. An unavailable or
inapplicable field is #{\bot}.}

\p{The equivalence class identifies only the ambiguity allowed by the declared
regime: #{(A,\nu)} with #{UA=W^*}, #{\operatorname{rank}(A)=n'}, and
#{\nu(z)\in N(U)} in the linear regime;
no universal quotient, recorded as non-identifiable under the stated
assumptions, in the undercomplete regime; positive diagonal scaling and
permutation #{(D,P_\sigma)} in the axis-aligned regime; and the
classifier-visible null-space class #{N(A)=N(A^*)} in the sparse regime. A
sparse decoder has permutation-and-scaling ambiguity only when that convention
is declared and measured. No scalar summary is defined, and none of these
fields alone establishes monosemanticity.}

\p{\strong{Source-grounded finite instance.} In the compressed-sensing arm,
the paper fixes #{n=16}, #{n'=m=8}, #{|Y|=64}, draws a Gaussian measurement
matrix with normalized columns, and tests #{k\in\{1,2,3\}} over ten seeds. For
the #{k=1} arm, the record declares discriminative sparse recovery and the
#{W^*=U^*A^*} and spark conditions; it reports #{R^2_{\rm sparse}\approx1}
for supervised Lasso, #{R^2_{\rm sparse}\approx n'/n=0.5} for OLS, and
#{\operatorname{MCC}_{\rm sparse}\approx0.86} for unsupervised Lasso, with
#{\operatorname{MCC}_{\rm axis}=\bot} and #{\operatorname{MMU}_{\rm cross}=\bot}.
The ten-seed estimate, decoder arm, and missing fields are part of #{\mathsf E};
the values are finite synthetic observations, not a deployment or capability
claim.}

\p{\strong{FTIP deduction.} This record makes the representation question
conditional on capacity, causal direction, and an explicit equivalence class.
It lets the existing checkpoint route report linear recoverability, axis
alignment, sparse recovery, and cross-system matching without treating any one
readout as a capability or deployment result. A finite observation can support
a regime-specific comparison; it cannot identify a developmental mechanism or
transfer beyond the declared task and assumptions.}

\p{\strong{Proposed protocol.} Before inspecting a checkpoint, freeze the task
family, latent or proxy labels, split, width ratio, causal regime, assumptions,
decoder class, comparison model and layer for MMU, stimulus set, and held-out
evaluator. Select layers or decoder settings on validation data only. Report
the full record, seed repetitions, missingness, and assumption-violation arms;
keep each #{R^2}, MCC, and MMU in its native units. For real model activations,
treat latent recovery as a declared proxy assay and report the proxy
construction with the same boundary.}

\p{A scoped local formalization search of Forest, formal-math, TauCetiReview,
and TauCetiWorker found no declaration that packages this four-regime record
with its separately typed readouts. Existing representation and evaluation
declarations cover neighboring fields only, so this finite identifiability
record remains a protocol proposal.}
